  "stream": true,
  "system": "You are AIOS, a coding agent. Your working directory is <CWD>.\n\nUse the read tool — not shell commands like cat — to inspect text files. Use offset and limit to continue reading large files.\n\nCheck the [exit code: N] marker on every bash result; investigate failures before moving on.\n\nTrack every background job id you start. You are notified in-session when a job finishes — do not busy-poll or sleep on one; keep working on independent steps and do not duplicate a running job's work. Before giving a final answer, collect every still-relevant job with job_output (set wait: true only when you are genuinely blocked on it), and job_kill jobs that stopped mattering.\n\nRead an existing file before overwriting it with write; prefer edit for targeted changes.\n\nRead a file before editing it, unless you just created or edited it in this session.\n\nPrefer apply_patch over separate edit calls for changes across files or separated regions. Read a file before updating, deleting, or moving it, unless you just created or edited it in this session. The whole patch is checked first and a rejected patch changes nothing; once applying starts, operations run in order, stop at the first failure, and are not rolled back, so re-read affected files before retrying when needed.",
  "thinking": {
    "type": "enabled"
  },
  "tools": [
    {
      "description": "Read a UTF-8 text file and return line-numbered content.",
      "input_schema": {
        "properties": {
          "file_path": {
            "description": "Path to read, resolved by the filesystem backend.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "limit": {
            "description": "Maximum number of lines to return. Defaults to 2000.",
            "type": "number"
          },
          "offset": {
            "description": "1-based first line to return. Defaults to 1.",
            "type": "number"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "file_path"
        ],
        "type": "object"
      },
      "name": "read"
    },
    {
      "description": "Find files, not directories, whose paths match a glob pattern, including hidden and ignored files. Returns up to 100 paths in modification-time order; a larger result keeps the first paths.",
      "input_schema": {
        "properties": {
          "path": {
            "description": "Directory to search in. Defaults to the session workspace; a relative path resolves against it.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "pattern": {
            "description": "Glob pattern to match file paths against (e.g. \"**/*.ts\", \"src/**/*.test.js\"). A pattern with no \"/\" matches the basename at any depth, so \"*\" and \"*.ts\" both search the whole tree; include a separator to anchor the depth.",
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "pattern"
        ],
        "type": "object"
      },
      "name": "glob"
    },
    {
      "description": "Search file contents with a ripgrep regular expression. Returns matching lines with line numbers, grouped by file. Returns up to 250 matches.",
      "input_schema": {
        "properties": {
          "include": {
            "description": "One glob filter for which files to search (e.g. \"*.ts\", \"*.{js,jsx}\"). Not a list; negation is not supported.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "path": {
            "description": "File or directory to search. Defaults to the session workspace; a relative path resolves against it.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "pattern": {
            "description": "Regular expression to search for (ripgrep syntax).",
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "pattern"
